          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Light signifies God’s holy, life-giving presence, truth, and salvation—supremely in Christ—with derivative applications to divine law, moral righteousness, and believers’ visible witness.\",\"book_summary\":\"Light is God’s law embodied in publicly visible acts of obedience that serve as an example to others.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine truth and goodness, including their manifestation through righteous guidance and witness.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Visible righteous obedience embodying divine law.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book confines light to the intersection of two recognized applications—law as guidance and believers’ observable good works. It excludes or leaves unrepresented the consensus’s broader cases in which light directly identifies God, Christ, revelation, salvation, life, or glory without reference to visible human obedience.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no necessary contradiction: visible obedience is a standard derivative instance of biblical light symbolism. The disagreement arises only if the book intends this restricted identification to exhaust the symbol’s meaning.\",\"rationale\":\"Proverbs 6:23 directly associates light with law, while Matthew 5:16 associates shining light with good works visible to others. The book synthesizes these established strands into a more specific identification. Because the consensus expressly recognizes both divine law and believers’ righteous witness, the reading is not novel or referentially opposed to consensus, but it materially narrows the symbol’s extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The two citations independently support law as light and visible good works as shining light, but neither explicitly states that light universally means law embodied in visible obedience; the composite and potentially exclusive definition is therefore narrower than either text requires.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
